139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 469 0 R
/E (In this Arduino program, a potentiometer is connected to analog pin A0 \(potPin\) and a DC motor is connected to PWM pin 5 \(motorPin\) through a motor driver or transistor.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
140 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 470 0 R
/E (The setup\(\) function runs once when the Arduino starts. It sets the motor pin as an OUTPUT so the Arduino can control the motor's speed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 471 0 R
/E (The loop\(\) function runs continuously. It reads the position of the potentiometer using analogRead\(\), which gives a value between 0 and 1023 depending on how much the knob is rotated.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 472 0 R
/E (The map\(\) function converts this value to a range of 0 to 255, which is required for PWM \(Pulse Width Modulation\). The analogWrite\(\) function then sends this PWM signal to the motor, controlling its speed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 473 0 R
/E (So overall, rotating the potentiometer changes the speed of the DC motor. Turning the knob one way slows the motor down, while turning it the other way increases the motor's speed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
